        <h1>7 Careless Mistakes That Cost O-Level Maths Marks &mdash; And the SHARP Fix</h1>
        <div class="blog-post-meta">
          <a class="blog-author" href="/about/" rel="author">By Jeremy Lim, Founder</a>
          <span>&middot;</span>
          <time datetime="2026-05-27">27 May 2026</time>
          <span>&middot;</span>
          <span class="blog-reading-time">7 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-post-body">
        <p>In O-Level Mathematics, most of the marks your child loses come from <em>method</em>, not from gaps in understanding. They pick the wrong approach, skip working, round too early, or freeze on a question type they can actually do. The GCE O-Level Mathematics (4052) paper rewards clear, step-by-step working, and it punishes the small slips a tired student repeats under exam pressure. Each of those slips has a routine that fixes it.</p>
        <p>At A-Worthy we teach the SHARP Method, five steps for every question: <strong>S</strong>ee the question type, <strong>H</strong>it the right framework, <strong>A</strong>pply it correctly, <strong>R</strong>efine through checking, <strong>P</strong>ractise via retrieval. At the Hit step your child picks a named framework matched to the topic (READ, LABEL, PLOT, DATA, RULE, SHOW), so they know which tool to use. Below are the seven mistakes we see most often in students&rsquo; work, and the fix for each one.</p>

        <h2>1. Starting the working before decoding the question</h2>
        <p>The most expensive habit in O-Level Maths is calculating before reading carefully. A student sees an equation and starts solving, when the command word was actually &ldquo;simplify&rdquo;, or the question wanted the answer left &ldquo;in the form <em>a</em>&radic;<em>b</em>&rdquo;, or correct to a set number of decimal places. The mathematics is right, but the answer scores zero because it doesn&rsquo;t match what was asked.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix (See + READ):</strong> Before writing anything, underline the command word (solve, simplify, factorise, express, show) and circle the required answer form and accuracy. For word problems, the READ framework (Read, Extract, Assign, Derive) makes your child pull out the quantities and assign variables <em>before</em> writing a single equation. Thirty seconds of careful reading saves a whole question of wasted working.</p>

        <h2>2. Doing too much in your head and losing method marks</h2>
        <p>Cambridge examiners award <em>method</em> marks as well as answer marks. A student who reaches the wrong final number through correct, clearly shown working still earns most of the marks. A student who does three steps in their head and writes only the final (wrong) answer earns nothing. Mental arithmetic under pressure is also where careless slips pile up.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix (one operation per line):</strong> Build the habit of writing one operation per line, with nothing skipped. It feels slower when you practise, but it turns out faster in the exam, because checking becomes easy and the method marks are banked even when the final answer slips. On a paper full of multi-step questions, that habit alone protects a lot of marks.</p>

        <h2>3. Reaching for <span class="nobr">SOH-CAH-TOA</span> on a non-right-angled triangle</h2>
        <p>Trigonometry is where students most often pick the wrong method: using <span class="nobr">SOH-CAH-TOA</span> on a triangle with no right angle, or the sine rule when the cosine rule was needed. The working looks confident and earns nothing.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix (RULE):</strong> Run a right-angle check first. If the triangle is right-angled, use <span class="nobr">SOH-CAH-TOA</span>. If not, decide between the cosine rule (you have two sides and the included angle) and the sine rule (you have a matching side-and-angle pair). Label sides and angles with the standard convention, execute, then sense-check: is the angle acute or obtuse as expected? Is the bearing measured clockwise from north?</p>

        <h2>4. Rounding too early and dropping the units</h2>
        <p>Rounding an intermediate value to 3 significant figures and feeding it into the next step carries the error forward, and the final answer drifts outside the accepted range. On the O-Level paper, unless a question says otherwise, final answers go to 3 significant figures (angles in degrees to 1 decimal place), but <em>intermediate</em> values should keep full calculator precision. Forgetting units (cm, cm&sup2;, degrees) is a separate and entirely avoidable way to drop marks.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix (round once, at the end):</strong> Keep full precision throughout (use the calculator&rsquo;s answer memory), round only the final value, and write the unit every time. Make &ldquo;number + unit + correct accuracy&rdquo; an automatic final step before moving on.</p>

        <h2>5. Stating geometry answers with no reasons</h2>
        <p>Angle and geometry questions require a <em>reason</em> for each step, such as &ldquo;vertically opposite angles&rdquo;, &ldquo;angles in the same segment&rdquo; or &ldquo;tangent perpendicular to radius&rdquo;. A correct angle with no stated property loses the reasoning marks, and a chain of unjustified angles can unravel completely.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix (LABEL):</strong> Redraw the figure and Label every given measurement, Apply the relevant property, Build the chain of angles one justified step at a time, Execute, and Link back to what the question asked. Stating the property on every line is the difference between full marks and half marks on geometry questions.</p>

        <h2>6. Trying to do statistics by mental arithmetic</h2>
        <p>Statistics questions (mean from a frequency table, median from cumulative frequency, standard deviation) reward tidy organisation, not clever mental maths. Students who skip the table almost always drop method marks, even when they happen to land the right number.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix (DATA):</strong> Define which measure is required, Arrange the data into a frequency or cumulative-frequency table, Tabulate the intermediate working clearly (&Sigma;<em>fx</em> for the mean, cumulative frequencies for the median), then Answer in context with the correct units. Examiners award method marks for correct tabulation even when the final arithmetic slips, so DATA protects that partial credit.</p>

        <h2>7. Freezing on &ldquo;show that&rdquo; and proof questions</h2>
        <p>Many students either skip &ldquo;show that&rdquo; questions or reason in a circle, assuming the result they&rsquo;re meant to prove. Because the answer is printed in the question, examiners want to see the reasoning at every step.</p>
        <p><strong>The fix (SHOW):</strong> State the result you need to reach and write it at the top so you know your destination. Hunt for the theorems, identities or algebraic properties that get you there. Order the logical chain from the given information to the target before you write. Then Write each line formally with a stated reason.</p>

        <h2>The pattern behind every fix</h2>
        <p>None of these seven fixes needs your child to be &ldquo;better at maths&rdquo;. Each is a routine. Five of them are named SHARP frameworks (READ, RULE, LABEL, DATA, SHOW), which give your child steps to follow instead of guessing. A student who knows the content but keeps scoring lower than expected usually has a technique gap, not a knowledge gap, and technique can be taught.</p>
        <p>That&rsquo;s how our O-Level Maths classes work. In a group of at most 6, your child learns the framework for each question type and uses it in homework. Their working comes back every week with written, marker-style feedback, so these habits are checked, not just explained. Doing more past papers without a routine usually just repeats the same mistakes.</p>
